families gather outside a prison in  myanmar's commercial capital yangon  hoping to be reunited with their loved  ones  the military junta freed 600 people  including criminals and foreign  nationals as part of an annual amnesty  to mark the buddhist new year but  political prisoners were not included  my daughter was arrested on the 25th of  april 2021 and sent for interrogation  she was sentenced to three years in  prison on a political charge now she's  been in prison for more than a year  the military seized power in february  last year just a few months after an  sansuchi's national league for democracy  swept to power  within days of the coup protesters were  on the streets demanding her release and  a return to civilian rule  a campaign of civil disobedience  followed which triggered a deadly  crackdown by security forces  since then thousands of protesters have  been arrested and jailed the military's  arrested an astonishing number of people  since the coup in february of last year  so there's a there's about 13 000 people  who are political prisoners in myanmar  they're in jail for any perceived  opposition to the coup so peaceful  protesters  if you carried a banner that that  suggested you weren't happy with the  coup you could be sentenced to a  considerable period of time in jail  human rights groups say the junta uses  political prisoners as bargaining chips  freeing them from time to time to  appease countries critical of the coup  for these families it means more  uncertainty not knowing when they'll see  their relatives again  victoria gatenbee al jazeera
 

